<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Williamsburg, Virginia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Williamsburg is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, between the James and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> rivers. It was the capital of the colony of Virginia from 1699 to 1780, when the government moved to Richmond, and is home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-william-and-mary">College of William &amp; Mary</a>. Its colonial centre, restored from the 1920s as Colonial Williamsburg, is a large living-history museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/williamsburg-virginia.jpg" alt="Williamsburg, Virginia" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ron Cogswell · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Governor%27s_Palace_--_Williamsburg_(VA)_September_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Williamsburg stands on a ridge on the Virginia Peninsula between the James and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> rivers, partly encircled by Queen's Creek and College Creek. It lies in the northern part of the Hampton Roads metropolitan area, about 45 miles south-east of Richmond. It is an independent city, legally independent of James City County to the west and south and of York County to the east, a status created by a new Virginia state constitution. It had a population of 15,425 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the English arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in 1607, the area was part of the Powhatan Confederacy. Between 1630 and 1633, after the war that followed the Indian massacre of 1622, the colonists built a defensive palisade across the peninsula, with a settlement called Middle Plantation as its guard-station. After Jamestown burned during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacons-rebellion">Bacon's Rebellion</a> in 1676, temporary government headquarters were set up at Middle Plantation, whose high ground the House of Burgesses found safer and more pleasant than humid, mosquito-plagued Jamestown. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-william-and-mary">College of William &amp; Mary</a> was established there in 1693, and the city of Williamsburg was founded on the site in 1699.</p><p>Williamsburg was the capital of the Colony and Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> from 1699 to 1780 and the centre of political events leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>. During the Revolutionary era <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> made up over 50 per cent of its population. In 1771 Lord Dunmore, the last royal governor, announced the first attempted canal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, across the peninsula; it was never completed. In the Civil War the college was closed in 1861, and its College Building, used as a Confederate barracks and then a hospital, was burned by Union forces in 1862.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city's economy is driven by Colonial Williamsburg, its restored Historic Area, a living history museum of the 18th-century colonial period. It includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>'s first capitol building, the Governor's Palace, Bruton Parish Church and the Peyton Randolph House. The Wren Building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-william-and-mary">College of William &amp; Mary</a>, at the head of Duke of Gloucester Street, was among the first buildings restored by W. A. R. Goodwin and the family of John D. Rockefeller Jr. as they began creating Colonial Williamsburg. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> and Yorktown, Williamsburg forms the Historic Triangle. William &amp; Mary is the second-oldest institution of higher education in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Williamsburg, Virginia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The capital moved to <b>Williamsburg</b> in 1699.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-virginia">Colony of Virginia</a></li></ul></section>
